edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488240 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $3\\cdot {5}^{s}=75.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "936aa682-294b-52d5-96a5-a38d57d9bb85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488240 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by dividing both sides by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{s} & =75 \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot {5}^{s}}{3} & =\\frac{75}{3} \\\\ {5}^{s} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $25$ as ${5}^{2}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{s} & =25 \\\\ {5}^{s} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{s} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ s & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $s=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488241 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${10}^{x}={10}^{5},$ then $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9744b183-f539-5606-a140-f9ad45bfd0fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488241 #edb/ref 1074 "Since both sides of the equation share the same base $(10)$, we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x} & ={10}^{5} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488260 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488260 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${4}^{x}=\\frac{1}{4},$ then $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5891ca3-f381-532b-913f-b6dc3bcab055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488260 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{4}$ as ${4}^{-1}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & ={4}^{-1} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488279 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation ${7}^{-3}={7}^{y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1e12ec8-496f-581a-aa45-273ef2ee6953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488279 #edb/ref 1074 "Since both sides of the equation share the same base $(7)$, we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{-3} & ={7}^{y} \\\\ -3 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488284 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488284 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488284 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488284 #edb/ref 1071 "If ${6}^{t}=\\frac{1}{36},$ then $t=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa2ae142-bf31-5242-a307-d8faa66809d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488284 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite $36$ as ${6}^{2}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{t} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {6}^{t} & =\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}} \\\\ {6}^{t} & ={6}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{t} & ={6}^{-2} \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
